WEBVTT

00:00.370 --> 00:01.960
- [Instructor] In this
video we're gonna study

00:01.960 --> 00:05.650
geometric series, and to
understand that I'm gonna construct

00:05.650 --> 00:07.380
a little bit of a table to understand

00:07.380 --> 00:11.327
how our money could grow if
we keep depositing, let's say,

00:11.327 --> 00:14.100
$1,000 a year in a bank account.

00:14.100 --> 00:17.560
So, let's say this is the year,

00:17.560 --> 00:19.220
and we're gonna think
about how much we have

00:19.220 --> 00:21.250
at the beginning of the year,

00:21.250 --> 00:26.040
and then this is the
dollars in our account.

00:26.040 --> 00:29.330
And let's say that the bank
is always willing to give us

00:29.330 --> 00:32.460
5% per year, which is pretty good.

00:32.460 --> 00:34.040
It's very hard to find a bank account

00:34.040 --> 00:37.210
that will actually give
you 5% growth per year.

00:37.210 --> 00:40.030
So that means if you put
$100 in at the end of a year,

00:40.030 --> 00:42.140
or exactly a year later it'd be $105.

00:42.140 --> 00:45.430
If you put $1,000 in, a
year later, it'd be 1,050.

00:45.430 --> 00:47.090
It'd be 5% larger.

00:47.090 --> 00:51.810
And so, let's say that we want
to put $1,000 in per year,

00:51.810 --> 00:53.540
and I want to think about, well,

00:53.540 --> 00:55.840
what is going to be my balance

00:55.840 --> 00:59.140
at the beginning of year one,
at the beginning of year two,

00:59.140 --> 01:01.340
at the beginning of
year three, and then see

01:01.340 --> 01:03.320
if we can come up with
a general expression

01:03.320 --> 01:07.050
for the beginning of year n.

01:07.050 --> 01:09.990
So, year one, right at
the beginning of the year,

01:09.990 --> 01:13.720
I put in $1,000 in the account.

01:13.720 --> 01:15.620
That's pretty straightforward.

01:15.620 --> 01:17.840
But then what happens in year two?

01:17.840 --> 01:22.150
I'm going to deposit $1,000,
but then that original $1,000

01:22.150 --> 01:23.790
that I have would have grown.

01:23.790 --> 01:27.020
So, I'm going to deposit $1,000,

01:27.020 --> 01:29.790
and then the original $1,000 that I put

01:29.790 --> 01:33.610
at the beginning of year one,
that has now grown by 5%.

01:33.610 --> 01:38.033
Growing by 5% is the same
thing as multiplying by 1.05.

01:39.490 --> 01:43.610
So, this is now going to be plus $1,000

01:43.610 --> 01:47.840
times 1.05.

01:47.840 --> 01:49.150
Fairly straightforward.

01:49.150 --> 01:51.240
Now, what about the
beginning of year three?

01:51.240 --> 01:52.860
How much would I have in the bank account

01:52.860 --> 01:56.060
right when I've made that
first, that year three deposit?

01:56.060 --> 01:56.893
Pause this video.

01:56.893 --> 01:58.260
See if you can figure that out.

02:00.170 --> 02:02.400
Well, just like at the
beginning of year two

02:02.400 --> 02:03.650
and the beginning of year one,

02:03.650 --> 02:06.870
we're going to make $1,000 deposit,

02:06.870 --> 02:11.610
but now the money from
year two has grown by 5%,

02:11.610 --> 02:16.610
so this is now going to
be $1,000 times 1.05.

02:18.040 --> 02:20.590
And then that money that
we originally deposited

02:20.590 --> 02:25.590
from year one, that was
1,000 times 1.05 in year two,

02:25.650 --> 02:28.080
that's going to grow by another 5%.

02:28.080 --> 02:30.990
And so, this is going to be plus 1,000

02:31.890 --> 02:35.710
times 1.05 times 1.05.

02:35.710 --> 02:37.830
We're growing by another 5%.

02:37.830 --> 02:39.600
Well, we could just rewrite this part

02:39.600 --> 02:43.760
right over here as 1.05 squared.

02:43.760 --> 02:45.410
So, do you see a general pattern

02:45.410 --> 02:47.340
that's going to happen here?

02:47.340 --> 02:49.870
Well, as you go to year n, in
fact, pause the video again,

02:49.870 --> 02:51.460
see if you can write a general expression.

02:51.460 --> 02:52.380
You're gonna have to do a little bit

02:52.380 --> 02:55.150
of this dot, dot, dot
action in order to do it.

02:55.150 --> 02:58.100
But see if you could write a
general expression for year n.

02:58.970 --> 03:03.333
Well, for year n, you're going
to make that original $1,000

03:05.230 --> 03:08.590
at the beginning of year n,
and then you're going to have

03:08.590 --> 03:12.660
1,000, 1,000, times 1.05

03:14.100 --> 03:15.800
for that $1,000 that you deposit

03:15.800 --> 03:18.360
at the beginning of year n minus one.

03:18.360 --> 03:21.980
And then this is just going to keep going,

03:21.980 --> 03:26.450
and it's going to go all
the way to plus $1,000

03:26.450 --> 03:31.450
to times 1.05 to the power

03:31.810 --> 03:34.420
of the number of years
you've been compounding.

03:34.420 --> 03:36.160
So, you could do this $1,000 as the one

03:36.160 --> 03:37.260
that you put in year one,

03:37.260 --> 03:40.230
and then how many years has it compounded?

03:40.230 --> 03:41.470
Well, when you go from one to two,

03:41.470 --> 03:42.680
you've compounded one year.

03:42.680 --> 03:45.110
When you go from one to three,
you've compounded two years.

03:45.110 --> 03:47.640
So, when we're talking about
the beginning of year n,

03:47.640 --> 03:50.320
you go up to the exponent
that is one less than that.

03:50.320 --> 03:54.360
And so, this is going to be
to the n minus one power.

03:54.360 --> 03:56.810
So, what we just did here
is we've just constructed

03:56.810 --> 03:58.720
each one of these when we're saying,

03:58.720 --> 04:00.560
okay, how much money do we
have in our bank account

04:00.560 --> 04:02.430
at the beginning of year three?

04:02.430 --> 04:03.880
Or how much do we have in our bank account

04:03.880 --> 04:05.720
at the beginning of year n?

04:05.720 --> 04:08.600
These are geometric series,

04:08.600 --> 04:11.293
and I will write that word down.

04:11.293 --> 04:16.293
Geo, geometric series.

04:16.570 --> 04:18.610
Now, just as a little bit of a review,

04:18.610 --> 04:20.970
or it might not be review,
it might be a primer,

04:20.970 --> 04:23.750
series are related to sequences,

04:23.750 --> 04:27.700
and you can really view
series as sums of sequences.

04:27.700 --> 04:30.210
Sequences, and let me go down a little bit

04:30.210 --> 04:33.340
so that you can, so we have
a little bit more space,

04:33.340 --> 04:36.350
a sequence is an ordered list of numbers.

04:36.350 --> 04:38.730
A sequence might be something like,

04:38.730 --> 04:42.160
well, let's say we have
a geometric sequence,

04:42.160 --> 04:44.780
and a geometric sequence, each successive

04:44.780 --> 04:48.560
term is the previous term
times a fixed number.

04:48.560 --> 04:49.700
So, let's say we start at two,

04:49.700 --> 04:51.610
and every time we multiply by three.

04:51.610 --> 04:54.570
So, we'll go from two,
two times three is six,

04:54.570 --> 04:59.170
six times three is 18,
18 times three is 54.

04:59.170 --> 05:03.040
This is a geometric sequence,
ordered list of numbers.

05:03.040 --> 05:06.890
Now, if we want to think
about the geometric series,

05:06.890 --> 05:09.090
or the one that's analogous to this,

05:09.090 --> 05:10.840
is that we would sum the terms here.

05:10.840 --> 05:15.840
So, this would be two
plus six plus 18 plus 54.

05:16.330 --> 05:18.310
Or we could even write it,
and this would look similar

05:18.310 --> 05:20.560
to what we had just done with
our little savings example

05:20.560 --> 05:24.480
is this is two plus two times three,

05:24.480 --> 05:27.330
plus two times three squared,

05:27.330 --> 05:32.000
plus two times three to the third power.

05:32.000 --> 05:34.310
And so, with the geometric series,

05:34.310 --> 05:38.600
you're going to have a sum
where each successive term

05:38.600 --> 05:42.020
in the expression is equal to,
if you put 'em all in order,

05:42.020 --> 05:44.430
is going to be equal to the term before it

05:44.430 --> 05:46.910
times a fixed amount.

05:46.910 --> 05:49.720
So, the second term is equal
to the first term times three,

05:49.720 --> 05:51.630
and we're summing them in a sequence.

05:51.630 --> 05:53.010
You're just looking at it.

05:53.010 --> 05:54.630
It's an ordered list, so to speak,

05:54.630 --> 05:58.270
but here you are actually
adding up the ordered list.

05:58.270 --> 06:02.810
So, what we just saw
in this example is one,

06:02.810 --> 06:05.560
what a geometric series is, but also,

06:05.560 --> 06:07.740
a famous example of how it's useful,

06:07.740 --> 06:09.210
and this is just scratching the surface.

06:09.210 --> 06:12.140
If you were to go further
in finance or in business,

06:12.140 --> 06:14.330
you'll actually see geometric series

06:14.330 --> 06:16.553
popping up all over the place.
